orage:27%"], "cpuSpeedLimit": null, "exitCode": 0, "launchWallSeconds": 15.104, "runIndex": 1, "textCheck": {"status": "PASS", "alphanumericCount": 1028, "distinctWords": 115, "taskTerms": true, "flags": [], "method": "the runner's generated text (parsers: textExtraction) + lexical screen; full text retained"}, "textOutputSHA256": "53ce9d9516bcc5546d028b97edc8df1b0c461be8a714ec8040827beb1a4693e2"}, "metrics": {"promptTokenCount": 1338, "generatedTokenCount": 255, "promptTokensPerSecond": 342.1994884910486, "decodeTokensPerSecond": 27.075812274368232, "firstTokenLatencyMS": 3910, "loadTimeSeconds": 1.569, "coldRun": true, "harnessStamp": "executorch-mac-v1-2026-10-08", "initialThermalState": "nominal", "finalThermalState": "nominal", "memoryPeakResidentMB": 3531.234375}, "harnessStamp": "executorch-mac-v1-2026-10-08", "decodedText": "<think>\nOkay, the user is asking for a list of 25 distinct things that on-device AI can do on a phone that a cloud model cannot. Let me start by understanding the difference between on-device AI and cloud AI.\n\nFirst, on-device AI processes data locally on the phone, which means it doesn't need to send data to the cloud. This has implications for privacy, latency, and connectivity. For example, if a phone is in an area with no internet, on-device AI can still function, whereas cloud AI would fail.\n\nI need to think of 25 distinct points. Let me brainstorm possible differences. Privacy is a big one because on-device AI doesn't send data. Then there's offline functionality, which is crucial for areas without internet. Also, real-time processing is better with on-device AI since it doesn't wait for cloud responses.\n\nAnother point could be local data processing for tasks like image recognition without needing to upload the image. Battery efficiency is another aspect since cloud processing uses more power. Also, on-device AI can handle tasks even when the phone is offline, which is a big advantage.\n\nI should also consider things like local language processing, which might be more accurate for the user's specific language. Maybe things like voice commands without internet", "outputSample": "<think>\nOkay, the user is asking for a list of 25 distinct things that on-device AI can do on a phone that a cloud model cannot. Let me start by understanding the difference between on-device AI and c", "provenance": {"definitions": "prefill tok/s = prompt_tokens / (prompt_eval_end_ms - inference_start_ms) * 1000. The window opens before the runner tokenizes the prompt (TextLLMRunner::generate sets inference_start_ms, then encodes), so prefill includes tokenization.\nTTFT ms = first_token_ms - inference_start_ms = the same window (first_token_ms is taken when prefill returns, before the first token is decoded to text).\ndecode tok/s = generated_tokens / (inference_end_ms - prompt_eval_end_ms) * 1000, generated_tokens = tokens of the decode loop; the reply has one more token, the one prefill sampled.\ncold = one generation in a fresh llama_main process (no --warmup). warm = llama_main --warmup: one unmeasured generation of the same prompt and max_new_tokens in the same process, stats and KV position reset, the second generation measured.\nClock: integer milliseconds (extension/llm/runner/util.h time_in_ms).", "recipe": "Qwen3-4B-ET1.5.1-xnnpack-8da4w-emb8-ctx2048.recipe.json", "recipeSha256": "937aeb90071174157a165d07a067e5e50febfef815f875479d75620a58487a47", "recipeAlias": "et1.5.1-xnnpack-8da4w-g128-emb8", "exporter": "extension/llm/export/export_llm (examples/models/qwen3/config/qwen3_xnnpack_q8da4w.yaml)", "modelProvenance": "own export with the ExecuTorch tag's own exporter (recipe.json beside the .pte); not a published artifact", "promptFile": "Qwen3-4B-ET1.5.1-xnnpack-8da4w-emb8-ctx2048.prompts/long-context-1024-gen256.chat.txt", "promptSha256": "360771899cd15f27bcedd5911f5c7f6775ccc15a092e2d133ec29cd83c90baf2", "tokenizer": "tokenizer.json", "tokenizerSha256": "aeb13307a71acd8fe81861d94ad54ab689df773318